										<content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>You&#039;re staring at a queue that won&#039;t clear. Your best agents are answering the same password, order status and refund questions for the tenth time today, while a furious customer with a complicated billing dispute has been on hold for twenty minutes. Your board wants you to cut cost per contact. Your customers want an answer in seconds, at midnight, in their own words. And someone on your team has just forwarded you a vendor deck promising that <strong>AI customer service</strong> will fix all of it.</p>
<p>That promise is partly true and partly dangerous. AI can resolve routine work instantly and help your agents work faster. It can also misunderstand people, repeat itself, and trap customers who need a human. The difference isn&#039;t the model you buy. It&#039;s how you define success. If you judge AI by <strong>end-to-end resolution</strong>, by how well it escalates, and by whether the customer has to repeat themselves, you&#039;ll build something customers trust. If you judge it only by how many chats never reach a human, you&#039;ll build a very efficient wall.</p>
<h2 id='table-of-contents'  id="boomdevs_1" >Table of Contents</h2>
<ul>
<li><a href="#what-ai-customer-service-is-and-why-it-matters-now">What AI Customer Service Is and Why It Matters Now</a><ul>
